  "overview": {
    "context": "Local PlayFab catalogue mapping",
    "summary": "This is a fictional card-game catalogue connected to a PlayFab-shaped item list. It helps a designer map the reward names used by a game service onto the cards in the simulation. The underlying example still uses packs, duplicate shards and crafting; connecting a catalogue does not import live reward chances or prices.",
    "distinctive": "The adapter changes item identities and displayed names, not probabilities. Source items are mapped deliberately so a catalogue refresh cannot silently become a new reward model.",
    "strength": "Consistent item names make it easier for designers and engineers to discuss the same card. Mapping is a workflow improvement rather than a new player-facing reward mechanic.",
    "analysis": "Check that intended cards map to the correct items, then compare their reward rules in the Models tab. Treat missing mappings as a content problem, not a reason to guess drop chances.",
    "popularity": "Microsoft documents PlayFab Economy as a service for items, currencies and stores. This specific card game and catalogue are synthetic, with no player reception or business results attached.",
    "business": "A catalogue can support commercial items and offers, but this mapping example does not reproduce store revenue, transactions or ownership. Business performance still depends on the game, its offers and verified company data.",
    "sources": [
      {
        "title": "Microsoft: PlayFab Economy overview",
        "url": "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/gaming/playfab/economy-monetization/economy-what-is"
      }
    ]
  }
